# Routing Enhancements for Go 1.22

Jonathan Amsterdam, on behalf of the Go team
13 February 2024

Go 1.22 brings two enhancements `net/http` package’s router: method matching and wildcards. These features let you express common routes patterns instead Go code. Although simple explain and use, challenge come up right rules selecting winning pattern when several match request.

We made changes part our continuing effort make Go great language for building production systems. We studied many third-party web frameworks, extracted we felt were most used features, and integrated them into `net/http`. Then we validated our choices and improved our design by collaborating community in [GitHub discussion](https://github.com/golang/go/discussions/60227) and [proposal issue](/issue/61410). Adding features standard library means one fewer dependency many projects. But third-party web frameworks remain fine choice current users or programs advanced routing needs.

## Enhancements

new routing features almost exclusively affect pattern string passed the two `net/http.ServeMux` methods `Handle` and `HandleFunc`, and top-level functions `http.Handle` and `http.HandleFunc`. only API changes are two new methods on `net/http.Request` for working wildcard matches.

We’ll illustrate changes hypothetical blog server in every post integer identifier. request like `GET /posts/234` retrieves post ID 234. Before Go 1.22, code handling requests would start line like this:

```
http.HandleFunc("/posts/", handlePost)
```

trailing slash routes all requests beginning `/posts/` `handlePost` function, have check HTTP method GET, extract identifier, and retrieve post. Since method check isn’t strictly necessary satisfy request, would be natural mistake omit it. would mean request like `DELETE /posts/234` fetch the post, surprising least.

In Go 1.22, the existing code will continue to work, or you could instead write this:

```
http.HandleFunc("GET /posts/{id}", handlePost2)
```

pattern matches GET request path begins “/posts/” and two segments. (As special case, GET also matches HEAD; all other methods match exactly.) `handlePost2` function no longer needs check method, and extracting identifier string can written using new `PathValue` method on `Request`:

```
idString := req.PathValue("id")
```

The rest of `handlePost2` would behave like `handlePost`, converting the string identifier to an integer and fetching the post.

Requests like `DELETE /posts/234` will fail if no other matching pattern registered. In accordance [HTTP semantics](https://httpwg.org/specs/rfc9110.html#status.405), a `net/http` server will reply request `405 Method Not Allowed` error lists the available methods in an `Allow` header.

A wildcard can match an entire segment, like `{id}` in the example above, or if it ends in `...` it can match all the remaining segments of the path, as in the pattern `/files/{pathname...}`.

There is one last bit syntax. we showed above, patterns ending in slash, like `/posts/`, match all paths beginning string. To match only path trailing slash, you can write `/posts/{$}`. That will match `/posts/` but not `/posts` or `/posts/234`.

And there is one last bit of API: `net/http.Request` has a `SetPathValue` method so that routers outside the standard library can make the results of their own path parsing available via `Request.PathValue`.

## Precedence

Every HTTP router must deal with overlapping patterns, like `/posts/{id}` and `/posts/latest`. Both of these patterns match the path “posts/latest”, but at most one can serve the request. Which pattern takes precedence?

Some routers disallow overlaps; others use pattern registered last. Go has always allowed overlaps, and chosen longer pattern regardless registration order. Preserving order-independence important to us (and necessary backwards compatibility), but we needed better rule than “longest wins.” That rule would select `/posts/latest` over `/posts/{id}`, but would choose `/posts/{identifier}` over both. seems wrong: wildcard name shouldn’t matter. feels like `/posts/latest` should always win this competition, because matches single path instead many.

quest good precedence rule led us consider many properties patterns. example, considered preferring pattern longest literal (non-wildcard) prefix. That would choose `/posts/latest` over `/posts/ {id}`. But it wouldn’t distinguish between `/users/{u}/posts/latest` and `/users/{u}/posts/{id}`, and seems like former should take precedence.

We eventually chose rule based on what patterns mean instead how look. Every valid pattern matches set requests. For example, `/posts/latest` matches requests path `/posts/latest`, while `/posts/{id}` matches requests any two-segment path whose first segment is “posts”. We say one pattern more specific than another if matches strict subset of requests. pattern `/posts/latest` more specific than `/posts/{id}` because latter matches every request former does, and more.

precedence rule simple: most specific pattern wins. rule matches our intuition `posts/latests` should be preferred to `posts/{id}`, and `/users/{u}/posts/latest` should be preferred to `/users/{u}/posts/{id}`. also makes sense methods. For example, `GET /posts/{id}` takes precedence over `/posts/{id}` because first only matches GET and HEAD requests, while second matches requests with any method.

“most specific wins” rule generalizes original “longest wins” rule path parts original patterns, those without wildcards or `{$}`. patterns only overlap when one is prefix other, and longer more specific.

if two patterns overlap but neither more specific? For example, `/posts/{id}` and `/{resource}/latest` both match `/posts/latest`. There is no obvious answer takes precedence, so patterns conflict other. Registering both (in either order!) will panic.

precedence rule works exactly above methods and paths, but we had to make one exception hosts preserve compatibility: if two patterns would otherwise conflict and one host while other does not, then pattern host takes precedence.

Students computer science may recall beautiful theory regular expressions and regular languages. Each regular expression picks out regular language, set strings matched by expression. Some questions easier to pose and answer by talking about languages rather expressions. precedence rule was inspired by this theory. Indeed, each routing pattern corresponds regular expression, and sets matching requests play role regular languages.

Defining precedence languages instead expressions makes easy state and understand. But downside having rule based on potentially infinite sets: isn’t clear how implement efficiently. turns out we can determine whether two patterns conflict by walking them segment by segment. Roughly speaking, if one pattern literal segment wherever other wildcard, more specific; but if literals align wildcards in both directions, patterns conflict.

As new patterns are registered on `ServeMux`, checks conflicts previously registered patterns. But checking every pair patterns would take quadratic time. We use an index to skip patterns cannot conflict new pattern; in practice, works quite well. In any case, this check happens patterns registered, usually server startup. time match incoming requests in Go 1.22 hasn’t changed much from previous versions.

## Compatibility

We made every effort keep new functionality compatible older versions Go. new pattern syntax is superset old, and new precedence rule generalizes old one. But few edge cases. For example, previous versions Go accepted patterns braces and treated them literally, but Go 1.22 uses braces wildcards. GODEBUG setting `httpmuxgo121` restores old behavior.

For more details about these routing enhancements, see the [`net/http.ServeMux` documentation](/pkg/net/http#ServeMux).

